Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 22:45:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] =?UTF-8?q?feat(vis):=20VIS-06=20=E2=80=94=20the=20flo?=
 =?UTF-8?q?or=20is=20made=20of=20boards,=20and=20it=20moves?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Answering two things directly: make the assembly come to life, and
distinguish the patterns and the species.

THE WEAR LAYER IS NOW AN ACTUAL FLOOR

It was one photograph stretched over a rectangle. It is now individual
boards, laid in whichever of the four patterns this company lays —
Straight Plank, Diagonal, Herringbone, Chevron — in any of the five
species in the catalogue, with every board cut from a different part of
the same photographed crop so no two are the same piece of wood.

The patterns are laid board by board rather than drawn as a repeating
texture, so herringbone really is interlocking L-pairs whose courses
STEP, and chevron really is mitred point-to-point with its apexes
lining up into rows. That is the difference most people cannot name and
can always see, and it is why the two are separate generators.

AND IT IS NEVER STILL

The stack drifts on a 26s cycle; the five layers float against it on
11/13/17/19/23/29s cycles that do not divide into it, so the assembly
never returns to the same pose twice inside a visit. Slow and weighted,
because that is how a floor moves — wood settles and breathes, it does
not spin.

A GEOMETRY BUG WORTH RECORDING

The first herringbone tiled with a repeating band of holes through it
that looked exactly like a generation-range bug. It was not. The wear
layer is a 1.55 rectangle, so one percent of x and one percent of y are
different physical lengths in it — `rotate(45deg)` does not come out at
45 degrees and two boards meant to meet at a mitre miss each other.
Widening the loops made it denser and still wrong. The board field is
now SQUARE, sized from the plane's width and clipped by it, and inside
that square the mitres land.

PERFORMANCE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED

  baseline, nothing animating          ~61 fps
  straight     (61 boards)  animating  ~42 fps
  chevron     (275 boards)  animating  ~40 fps
  herringbone (440 boards)  animating  ~40 fps

Seven times the boards costs two frames per second, because ONLY the
stack and the five layers animate — the boards ride as one already
rasterised composited layer. What does cost real money is compositing:
a `mix-blend-mode` over a moving ancestor and a `backdrop-filter` on
the glass layer were together worth 23% of the frame rate (35 -> 43fps,
same board counts, software rasteriser). Both are gone — the warm cast
is multiplied into each board's own background, where it is rasterised
once and never again — and a test now fails if either comes back.

Nothing animates off screen at all: `animation-play-state` is `paused`
in the stylesheet and only the IntersectionObserver sets it running,
clearing it again on exit. A visitor who never scrolls this far pays
nothing. Under prefers-reduced-motion the keyframes are removed outright
rather than paused.

BOTH THINGS MADE POSSIBLE

`variant="hero"` is built and works: the same assembly as a full-bleed
backdrop with the legend collapsed, for use above the fold. The homepage
does not pass it, because the hero is the LCP element and this would
become it — but the switch is now one word in home-client.tsx instead of
an argument. A test asserts the homepage is not currently using it, so
the choice stays deliberate.

framer-motion still has no importers, and the reason is no longer taste:
a declarative motion library server-renders its INITIAL state into the
style attribute, so a scroll-triggered reveal ships the COLLAPSED stack
in the HTML and anything that does not run JavaScript — several of the
answer engines this site is written for included — sees a flat
rectangle. It also cannot express an animation that is paused until
scrolled to without running a JavaScript frame loop, which is exactly
the cost avoided above.

THE F-205 GUARD IS NOW A REAL TEST, NOT A GREP

`26026+ Years in Toronto` reached production because three copies of one
number concatenated in the served HTML, and no source-level assertion
can see that. The suite now SERVER-RENDERS this component with
react-dom/server — already present, no new dependency, no vitest config
change — and COUNTS every string it publishes. Five layer names, five
descriptions, five questions, five species and four pattern names, each
exactly once; no text inside any animated element; one texture request
for all 440 boards; neither the collapsed nor the live state in the
server HTML.

ALSO FIXES verify:hygiene

ECOWOODS_VIS_05_floor_assembly.patch was committed to the repository
root through the web UI and tracked, which is what the failing guard was
reporting. Removed here. A patch is a transport format; the history has
the change.

VERIFICATION

  66 of 66 guards pass locally except verify:css, which cannot run in
  this sandbox (postcss unresolvable, npm 403) and fails identically on
  a clean checkout. The stylesheet was instead parsed by Chromium's
  CSSOM, and the animation verified in a real browser: play-state
  `paused` before the stage goes live and `running` after, the stack
  transform measurably different 2.5s apart, layer 02 floating from
  4.3px to 9.7px of Z.

  351 assertions. Eight mutations introduced and each one caught: text
  inside a layer, a dropped --fa-t fallback, an id-keyed handoff, a
  species with no crop, herringbone generated flat, Math.random in the
  geometry, the blend mode restored, and the hero variant mounted on the
  homepage.

  Two of those assertions were themselves wrong first and are recorded
  in the file: three searched for `Math.random`, `mix-blend-mode` and
  `backdrop-filter` and found all three — in the comments explaining why
  the code avoids them; and the pause assertion passed when the pause
  was deleted from `.fa-stack`, because `.fa-layer` still had one.

  parse-scan clean on 625 files. Resolving typecheck clean.

